; link/pop_cels_pg.link — ONE ROTATING PAGE of the split cel image (P3.78).
;
; Used once per page, with a different object and a different output each time. EVERY
; page links at the SAME address, and that is the point rather than an oversight: only
; one of them is mapped at a time, through $FFA7, so they all occupy $E000 and the beat
; schedule decides which physical block is showing.
;
; That is also exactly why they are separate LINK UNITS and not sections of one image —
; five things cannot occupy one address inside a single link.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; $E000..$FDFF — 7,680 B, NOT 8,192.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; gfx.s:178 sets $FF90 with MC3=1, which holds $FE00-$FEFF as constant RAM (it is where
; disk_read.s lands its NMI handler and its motor flag, precisely so they survive an MMU
; remap), and $FF00-$FFFF is always I/O. So the top 512 B of whichever block sits at
; $FFA7 CANNOT BE REACHED through the window. A page that overran into it would not fail
; at link time — it would write cel data at addresses the CPU answers from somewhere
; else entirely. harness/tools/cel_table.py checks each page's linked LENGTH against
; 7,680 and fails the build; that check is the only thing standing between a packing
; error and the silent-garbage failure mode the bank guard was built for.
;
; The first two bytes of every page are its own SIGNATURE. See the generated cel_pgN.s
; header, and char_draw.s's guard.
;
; The `load` value is bare hex for the reason link/pop_cels_res.link explains at length:
; the `$` form parses as zero, silently.

section prog load E000
